let and const are block-scoped; var is function-scoped; const prevents variable reassignment','let can be redeclared in the same block','var is block-scoped','const makes object properties deeply immutable','A'),(982,40,'During an evaluation of JavaScript Event Bubbling vs Event Capturing, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Bubbling propagates from target element up to document root; Capturing propagates down from root to target','Event delegation relies on disabling bubbling','Bubbling goes top-down; Capturing goes bottom-up','event.stopPropagation() triggers all parent listeners','A'),(983,40,'During an evaluation of JavaScript Promises and async/await, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Represent eventual completion/failure of asynchronous operations, avoiding callback hell','Can only resolve once per page reload','Block the main browser UI thread until the network responds','Create new OS kernel threads','A'),(984,40,'During an evaluation of JavaScript Event Loop, Call Stack, and Task/Microtask Queues, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Executes synchronous code on the call stack, then drains microtasks (Promises) before macrotasks (setTimeout)','setTimeout(fn, 0) runs synchronously before the next line','Macrotasks always execute before Promise microtasks','JavaScript executes on 16 parallel call stacks by default','A'),(985,40,'During an evaluation of JavaScript === (Strict Equality) vs == (Loose Equality), which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','=== compares both value and type without coercion; == performs implicit type coercion before comparing','0 === \'0\' evaluates to true','== compares both value and type strictly','=== converts strings to numbers automatically','A'),(986,40,'During an evaluation of RESTful API HTTP Methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE), which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','GET retrieves, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH partially updates, and DELETE removes a resource','PUT is used only for read-only queries','GET is used to delete database records','POST and PATCH are idempotent methods','A'),(987,40,'During an evaluation of Idempotency in HTTP methods, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Making multiple identical requests produces the same server state as a single request (GET, PUT, DELETE)','Only HTTPS requests can be idempotent','POST is idempotent by specification','Idempotent requests never return a response body','A'),(988,40,'During an evaluation of HTTP Cookies vs localStorage vs sessionStorage, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Cookies are sent with HTTP requests; localStorage persists across browser restarts; sessionStorage clears on tab close','Cookies can store up to 500 MB per domain','localStorage is sent in every HTTP request header','sessionStorage persists forever across all tabs','A'),(989,40,'During an evaluation of Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability and defense, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Attacker injects malicious scripts into web pages; prevented via context-aware output escaping and CSP','Only affects backend SQL databases without browsers','Attacker guesses the database root password','Prevented by disabling HTTPS','A'),(990,40,'During an evaluation of Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) vulnerability and defense, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Tricks an authenticated user\'s browser into sending unwanted requests; prevented via CSRF tokens and SameSite cookies','Only affects unauthenticated guest users','Steals CSS stylesheets from another domain','Prevented by using GET requests for state changes','A'),(991,40,'During an evaluation of SQL Injection (SQLi) in web applications and defense, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Untrusted input alters SQL query structure; prevented by using parameterized queries / prepared statements','Only affects NoSQL document stores','Prevented by validating input with client-side CSS','String concatenation with %s is immune to SQLi','A'),(992,40,'During an evaluation of CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing), which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Browser security mechanism using HTTP headers (Access-Control-Allow-Origin) to permit cross-origin requests','Encrypts HTML source code','A backend database replication protocol','A CSS animation property','A'),(993,40,'During an evaluation of JSON Web Token (JWT) structure, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Three Base64Url-encoded parts separated by dots: Header.Payload.Signature','Payload is encrypted by default without JWE','Encrypted binary file stored only in MySQL','Requires server-side session memory for every token','A'),(994,40,'During an evaluation of Responsive Web Design and Media Queries (@media), which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Adapts layout to different viewport sizes using fluid grids, flexible images, and CSS @media rules','Uses fixed pixel widths for all containers','Requires building a separate website for every screen resolution','Disables CSS on mobile devices','A'),(995,40,'During an evaluation of DOM (Document Object Model) manipulation, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Tree representation of an HTML document allowing JavaScript to query, modify, add, or remove nodes dynamically','Requires reloading the entire page for every text change','A backend SQL schema language','Cannot modify CSS styles at runtime','A'),(996,40,'During an evaluation of HTTP Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Prevents browsers and shared caches from storing or serving stale sensitive authenticated pages','Redirects HTTP to HTTPS','Caches the page publicly for 1 year','Compresses HTML using gzip','A'),(997,40,'During an evaluation of Password Hashing in Web Auth (PBKDF2, bcrypt, Argon2), which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Uses a cryptographic salt and key-stretching iterations so leaked password hashes resist rainbow-table and brute-force attacks','Passwords should be stored in plain text for recovery emails','Base64 encoding is sufficient for storing passwords','Plain MD5 without salt is recommended for passwords','A'),(998,40,'During an evaluation of Fetch API in modern JavaScript, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Promise-based API for making HTTP requests; resolves on HTTP 404/500 unless response.ok is checked','Cannot send JSON POST bodies','Automatically rejects the Promise on HTTP 404 status','Uses synchronous XMLHttpRequests on the main thread','A'),(999,40,'During an evaluation of JavaScript Closures, which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','A function that retains access to variables from its lexical outer scope even after the outer function has returned','A garbage collection pause','A method that closes the browser window','A syntax error caused by missing curly braces','A'),(1000,40,'During an evaluation of Flask Jinja2 Template Rendering (render_template), which option represents the standard behaviour or rule?','Renders dynamic HTML on the server and auto-escapes variables in {{ }} by default to mitigate XSS','Requires a separate Apache server to parse templates','Executes Python code directly inside the user\'s browser','Disables HTML escaping by default','A'),(1001,41,'Which of the following accurately describes A* Search Algorithm evaluation function f(n) = g(n) + h(n)?','g(n) is the actual cost from start to n; h(n) is the heuristic estimated cost from n to goal','g(n) is the heuristic estimate; h(n) is the depth','A* is complete and optimal even if h(n) overestimates the true cost','A* uses LIFO stack ordering','A'),(1002,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Admissible Heuristic in A* Search?','Never overestimates the true cost of reaching the goal (0 <= h(n) <= h*(n)), guaranteeing optimality','Always overestimates the cost to speed up search','Equals infinity for all nodes','Depends only on the branching factor','A'),(1003,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Minimax Algorithm with Alpha-Beta Pruning?','Prunes branches in two-player zero-sum games that cannot influence the final minimax decision','Changes the final chosen move compared to standard Minimax','Works only on single-agent shortest path problems','Requires training a neural network first','A'),(1004,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Supervised vs Unsupervised vs Reinforcement Learning?','Supervised uses labeled data; Unsupervised finds patterns in unlabeled data; RL learns via reward/penalty feedback','Supervised learning uses no target labels','K-Means clustering is a supervised regression algorithm','Linear regression is an unsupervised clustering algorithm','A'),(1005,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Overfitting vs Underfitting (Bias-Variance Tradeoff)?','Overfitting has low training error but high test error (high variance); Underfitting has high bias on both','Overfitting is solved by removing all training data','Underfitting occurs when the model is too complex','High variance means the model ignores the training data','A'),(1006,41,'Which of the following accurately describes L1 (Lasso) vs L2 (Ridge) Regularization?','L1 adds absolute weight penalty encouraging sparsity (feature selection); L2 adds squared weight penalty','L2 sets irrelevant feature weights to exact zero','Regularization increases overfitting on the training set','L1 and L2 are used only in K-Means clustering','A'),(1007,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Gradient Descent learning rate (alpha)?','Controls the step size taken opposite to the gradient of the loss function during parameter updates','If learning rate is too large, loss always converges smoothly','Gradient descent moves in the direction of steepest ascent of loss','Learning rate is the number of hidden layers','A'),(1008,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Logistic Regression and the Sigmoid Function?','Classification model mapping linear combinations to probabilities in (0, 1) via 1 / (1 + e^-z) using log-loss','A regression model that predicts unbounded continuous prices','Uses Mean Squared Error to guarantee a convex loss surface','Cannot output probabilities','A'),(1009,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Decision Tree splitting criteria (Entropy / Information Gain & Gini Impurity)?','Measures node purity to select the feature split that maximizes reduction in uncertainty/impurity','High entropy means all samples belong to a single class','Decision trees require feature scaling (z-score normalization)','Decision trees cannot overfit regardless of depth','A'),(1010,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Support Vector Machines (SVM) and the Kernel Trick?','Finds the maximum-margin hyperplane and implicitly maps inputs to higher-dimensional spaces via kernels (RBF, Polynomial)','Minimizes the margin between classes','Depends on every single training point equally rather than support vectors','Cannot handle non-linearly separable data','A'),(1011,41,'Which of the following accurately describes K-Nearest Neighbors (k-NN) algorithm?','Non-parametric instance-based learner that classifies a point by majority vote of its k closest training neighbors','Learns a compact weight vector during a long training phase and discards data','Is unaffected by irrelevant features or unscaled distances','k=1 always prevents overfitting on noisy data','A'),(1012,41,'Which of the following accurately describes K-Means Clustering algorithm?','Iteratively assigns points to the nearest of K centroids and recomputes centroids as cluster means','Automatically determines the optimal K without hyperparameter tuning','Finds arbitrary non-convex ring-shaped clusters easily','Is a supervised classification method','A'),(1013,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Principal Component Analysis (PCA)?','Orthogonal linear transformation projecting data onto directions (eigenvectors of covariance matrix) of maximum variance','Uses class labels to maximize class separability','Increases the number of features in the dataset','Does not require centering the data around zero','A'),(1014,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Precision, Recall, and F1-Score in classification?','Precision = TP/(TP+FP); Recall = TP/(TP+FN); F1 is the harmonic mean of Precision and Recall','Accuracy is the best metric for a 99.9% imbalanced fraud dataset','Recall measures false alarms among negative samples','F1-Score is the arithmetic mean of TP and TN','A'),(1015,41,'Which of the following accurately describes ROC Curve and AUC (Area Under the Curve)?','Plots True Positive Rate (Recall) vs False Positive Rate across classification thresholds; random classifier has AUC = 0.5','Plots Precision vs Epoch count','A model with AUC = 0.0 cannot be inverted','AUC depends on a single fixed probability threshold of 0.5','A'),(1016,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Vanishing Gradient Problem in Deep Neural Networks?','Gradients shrink exponentially through layers with Sigmoid/Tanh activations; mitigated by ReLU, BatchNorm, and residual connections','Caused by using ReLU activation functions','Occurs only in 1-layer linear models','Solved by initializing all weights to zero','A'),(1017,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Backpropagation algorithm in Neural Networks?','Computes gradients of the loss function with respect to weights efficiently using the chain rule of calculus','Updates input features while keeping weights fixed','Runs only during inference on test data','Does not require differentiable activation functions','A'),(1018,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) key operations?','Uses shared-weight convolutional filters (local receptive fields) and pooling layers for spatial hierarchy','Uses fully connected layers for every pixel without weight sharing','Cannot process 2D grid data like images','Pooling layers increase spatial resolution','A'),(1019,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Cross-Validation (k-Fold Cross-Validation)?','Partitions data into k folds, training on k-1 folds and validating on the held-out fold k times to estimate generalization','Trains on the test set directly to maximize accuracy','Uses the same fold for both training and validation simultaneously','Replaces the need for a loss function','A'),(1020,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Random Forest vs Gradient Boosting (XGBoost)?','Random Forest bags independent trees in parallel (reduces variance); Boosting builds sequential trees correcting prior residuals (reduces bias)','Random Forest builds trees sequentially where each tree fits residuals','Gradient Boosting cannot overfit if 10,000 trees are added','Both train only a single decision stump','A'),(1021,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Naive Bayes Classifier assumption?','Assumes features are conditionally independent given the class label, applying Bayes\' Theorem','Assumes features are 100% linearly correlated','Requires millions of epochs of gradient descent','Cannot be used for text spam classification','A'),(1022,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Bayes\' Theorem formula P(A|B)?','P(A|B) = [P(B|A) * P(A)] / P(B), relating posterior, likelihood, prior, and evidence','P(A|B) = P(A) + P(B)','P(A|B) is always equal to P(B|A)','P(A|B) ignores prior probabilities','A'),(1023,41,'Which of the following accurately describes Softmax Activation Function in multi-class classification?','Converts a vector of raw logits into a probability distribution over K classes that sums to 1.0','Outputs independent values between -1 and 1 that do not sum to 1','Is used exclusively in hidden layers 