1) - 1)','Cannot perform subtraction using an adder','A'),(1127,46,'Which of the following accurately describes IEEE 754 Single-Precision (32-bit) Floating Point format?','1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits (bias 127), and 23 fraction/mantissa bits (with implicit leading 1)','1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits, and 52 mantissa bits','Uses 2\'s complement for the exponent field','Has no representation for NaN or Infinity','A'),(1128,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Universal Logic Gates (NAND and NOR)?','Any Boolean function (AND, OR, NOT, XOR) can be implemented using only NAND gates or only NOR gates','AND and OR are universal gates without NOT','XOR alone can implement any Boolean function without constants','NAND is equivalent to OR followed by NOT','A'),(1129,46,'Which of the following accurately describes De Morgan\'s Theorems in Boolean Algebra?','NOT(A AND B) = (NOT A) OR (NOT B), and NOT(A OR B) = (NOT A) AND (NOT B)','NOT(A AND B) = (NOT A) AND (NOT B)','A + (B . C) = (A . B) + C','A XOR B = A AND B','A'),(1130,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Combinational vs Sequential Logic Circuits?','Combinational output depends only on current inputs (Adder, Mux); Sequential depends on current inputs and past state (Flip-Flops)','Multiplexer is a sequential circuit with memory','JK Flip-Flop is a combinational circuit','Combinational circuits require a clock signal','A'),(1131,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Program Counter (PC) and Instruction Register (IR)?','PC holds the memory address of the next instruction to fetch; IR holds the currently executing instruction','PC holds the result of ALU addition','IR holds the stack pointer address','Both are stored on the hard disk','A'),(1132,46,'Which of the following accurately describes RISC vs CISC Instruction Set Architectures?','RISC uses fixed-length instructions, load/store architecture, and many registers; CISC uses variable-length complex instructions','RISC allows arithmetic operations directly on memory operands','CISC cannot use instruction pipelining','x86 is a pure RISC architecture','A'),(1133,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Immediate, Direct, Indirect, and Indexed Addressing Modes?','Immediate: operand in instruction; Direct: address in instruction; Indirect: pointer in register/memory; Indexed: base + offset','Immediate mode fetches the operand from disk','Direct mode requires two memory accesses to fetch the operand','Register mode is the slowest addressing mode','A'),(1134,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Carry-Lookahead Adder (CLA) vs Ripple-Carry Adder?','CLA computes carry signals in parallel using Generate (G = AB) and Propagate (P = A xor B) to reduce propagation delay','Ripple-Carry Adder adds 64 bits in O(1) gate delay','CLA uses less hardware area than a Ripple-Carry Adder','CLA cannot add negative 2\'s complement numbers','A'),(1135,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Booth\'s Multiplication Algorithm?','Multiplies signed 2\'s complement numbers efficiently by encoding runs of 1s as a subtraction and addition','Works only on unsigned positive integers','Requires converting numbers to BCD first','Uses division to compute products','A'),(1136,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Unit?','Hardwired uses combinational logic/FSM (faster, RISC); Microprogrammed uses control memory storing microinstructions (flexible, CISC)','Microprogrammed control is faster than hardwired control','Hardwired control can be updated by changing a ROM table without redesigning gates','RISC processors exclusively use microprogrammed control','A'),(1137,46,'Which of the following accurately describes 5-Stage RISC Instruction Pipeline stages?','Instruction Fetch (IF) -> Instruction Decode (ID) -> Execute (EX) -> Memory Access (MEM) -> Write Back (WB)','Fetch -> Compile -> Link -> Load -> Execute','Decode -> Fetch -> Memory -> Execute -> Write Back','Pipelining reduces the latency of a single instruction to 1/5th','A'),(1138,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Structural, Data, and Control Hazards in Pipelining?','Structural: hardware resource conflict; Data: RAW/WAR/WAW dependency; Control: branch/jump instruction changes PC','Forwarding resolves all branch control hazards','RAW (Read After Write) is a false dependency solved by register renaming','Adding more pipeline stages eliminates branch penalties','A'),(1139,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Operand Forwarding (Bypassing) in a Pipeline?','Routes ALU output from EX/MEM or MEM/WB pipeline buffers directly to ALU inputs to avoid data hazard stalls','Eliminates the need for a load-use stall immediately after LW','Predicts branch outcomes at compile time','Writes results directly to the hard drive','A'),(1140,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Principle of Locality (Temporal and Spatial Locality)?','Temporal: recently accessed items likely accessed again soon; Spatial: nearby memory addresses likely accessed soon','Temporal locality justifies fetching multi-word cache lines','Spatial locality justifies keeping loop counters in registers','Random memory access exhibits maximal spatial locality','A'),(1141,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Direct-Mapped, Fully Associative, and Set-Associative Cache?','Direct-mapped: 1 block location (index = block mod N); Fully associative: any line; k-way Set-Associative: k lines per set','Direct-mapped cache has zero conflict misses','Fully associative cache requires no tag comparators','Set-associative cache cannot use LRU replacement','A'),(1142,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Cache Write Policies (Write-Through vs Write-Back)?','Write-Through updates both cache and main memory on every write; Write-Back updates only cache (dirty bit) until eviction','Write-Back writes to main memory on every CPU store instruction','Write-Through requires a dirty bit per cache line','Write-Back generates more memory bus traffic than Write-Through','A'),(1143,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) formula?','EMAT = Hit_Time + (Miss_Rate * Miss_Penalty)','EMAT = Hit_Rate * Miss_Penalty','EMAT = Miss_Time / Hit_Rate','EMAT is independent of the miss penalty','A'),(1144,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Programmed I/O, Interrupt-Driven I/O, and Direct Memory Access (DMA)?','DMA transfers blocks directly between I/O device and memory via a DMA controller without continuous CPU intervention','Programmed I/O frees the CPU to run other processes while polling','DMA transfers data through CPU general-purpose registers on every byte','Interrupt-Driven I/O has lower overhead than DMA for 1 GB disk transfers','A'),(1145,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Endianness (Big-Endian vs Little-Endian)?','Big-Endian stores the most significant byte (MSB) at the lowest memory address; Little-Endian stores the LSB at the lowest address','Little-Endian reverses the bits inside each individual byte','Network byte order (TCP/IP) is Little-Endian','x86-64 processors are Big-Endian only','A'),(1146,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Amdahl\'s Law for parallel speedup?','Speedup = 1 / ((1 - f) + (f / N)), where f is the parallelizable fraction and N is the number of processors','Speedup increases linearly to infinity even if 50% of the code is serial','Doubling processors always doubles total program speed','Applies only to disk storage capacity','A'),(1147,46,'Which of the following accurately describes CPU Performance Equation (Execution Time)?','CPU Time = Instruction Count (IC) * Cycles Per Instruction (CPI) * Clock Cycle Time','CPU Time = Clock Rate / (IC * CPI)','A higher clock rate (GHz) always guarantees lower CPU time regardless of CPI','CPI is always 1.0 for every instruction','A'),(1148,46,'Which of the following accurately describes JK Flip-Flop vs SR Flip-Flop?','JK Flip-Flop eliminates the invalid/indeterminate state (S=1, R=1) by toggling the output when J=1 and K=1','SR Flip-Flop toggles when S=1 and R=1','D Flip-Flop has two inputs D1 and D2','T Flip-Flop cannot be used in binary counters','A'),(1149,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Multiplexer (2^n-to-1 MUX) and Decoder (n-to-2^n)?','A 2^n-to-1 MUX selects 1 of 2^n data inputs using n select lines; an n-to-2^n Decoder activates 1 of 2^n outputs','An 8-to-1 MUX requires 8 select lines','A 3-to-8 Decoder has 8 input lines and 3 output lines','Multiplexers are sequential memory elements','A'),(1150,46,'Which of the following accurately describes Memory-Mapped I/O vs Isolated (Port-Mapped) I/O?','Memory-Mapped I/O shares the same address space and Load/Store instructions for both memory and I/O devices','Memory-Mapped I/O requires special IN and OUT assembly instructions','Isolated I/O reduces the available main memory address space','RISC architectures cannot use Memory-Mapped I/O','A'),(1151,47,'In the context of core technical principles, what is the defining property or complexity of 2\'s Complement representation of signed integers?','Has two representations for zero (+0 and -0)','Obtained by inverting all bits (1\'s complement) and adding 1; has a single representation for zero','Range for n bits is -(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)','Cannot perform subtraction using an adder','B'),(1152,47,'In the context of core technical principles, what is the defining property or complexity of IEEE 754 Single-Precision (32-bit) Floating Point format?','1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits, and 52 mantissa bits','1 sign bit, 8 exponent bits (bias 127), and 23 fraction/mantissa bits (with implicit leading 1)','Uses 2\'s complement for the exponent field','Has no representation for NaN or Infinity','B'),(1153,47,'In the context of core technical principles, what is the defining property or complexity of Universal Logic Gates (NAND and NOR)?','AND and OR are universal gates without NOT','Any 